!!id:"https://schemas.example.com/2026/32/app/orders-api-1.tn"
!!meta:"https://tson.io/2026/32/ltr8/http/meta-http-1.tn"
!!import:"https://schemas.example.com/2026/32/app/order-1.tn"
!!import:"https://schemas.example.com/2026/32/app/orders-errors-1.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/32/m/core.tn"
@doc:"""
  The orders service, described as types rather than as data about types.

  WORKS -- see sketch/README.md. Was blocked until the `data` base kind (UPSTREAM.md #15).

  Compare the data version this replaces (a !api document governed by api-1.tn): there, a body was
  { schema: uri  type: text } and nothing resolved either string. Here order, problem and
  sku_not_found are references the resolver follows through the imports above. The (schema, type)
  pair disappears because a type reference already carries identity.

  There is no title, version or envelope either. This document has an !!id, which gives it identity
  and §10 versioning, and @doc, which documents it. The envelope was re-inventing all three.

  An entry name IS the operationId, and unlike OpenAPI's it lives in a namespace with a collision
  rule, so two operations cannot quietly share one.
  """
{
  @doc:"""
    A page of results: the envelope OpenAPI hand-rolls per endpoint or bolts on with allOf, written
    once and applied. Templates work here -- this is the point of the probe in SketchTest.
    """
  page => <T> { items: [T]  next: uri?  total: int32 }

  @doc:"""
    The application is NAMED as an entry rather than written inline below, and that is forced rather
    than chosen. `page<order>` is schema syntax; an !operation payload is data, where a type_ref slot
    takes §5.6's positional form -- a bare token for an argument-free reference, and a braced record
    when arguments are present. So the inline spelling would be

      body: { name: page  arguments: [ { name: order } ] }

    which works and is checked (SketchTest pins both). Naming the application reads better, gives it
    an identity, and §8.2 dedupes it structurally. The one thing the braced form does better is the
    diagnostic: a bad argument there names the operation, where a bad argument in a named application
    is reported against the synthetic entry the template materialised.
    """
  order_page => page<order>

  list_orders => !operation {
    method:     GET
    path:       "/orders"
    parameters: []
    responses:  [
      !response { status: 200  body: order_page }
      !response { status: 400  body: problem }
    ]
  }

  create_order => !operation {
    method:     POST
    path:       "/orders"
    parameters: []
    request:    order
    responses:  [
      !response { status: 201  body: order }
      !response { status: 400  body: problem }
      !response { status: 404  body: sku_not_found }
    ]
  }
}
